{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem","id":859421,"title":"What's the \"point\" of politics in Requiem?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-04T20:16:54-07:00","id":859421,"title":"What's the \"point\" of politics in Requiem?","content":"Let me preface this whole topic by saying that this is not meant as a criticism, but rather something of a thought experiment. I adore Requiem and quite like all of the myriad factions and mythology that's been built up for it so far, and have no beef with it continuing.<br /> <br /> What's the &quot;point&quot; of the large focus on elaborate factions, games of reputation and rank, and all the other politics so central to the society of the undead that Requiem revolves around?<br /> <br /> Vampires are, at their core, hungry monsters who were once human, and hold to that former identity with varying degrees of dedication. Most of the vampires we see in folklore, legend, and fiction are largely solitary creatures, with the most famous (Dracula) being sire to four others, none of which he seems to care terribly much for. The immediate and obvious metaphors represented by vampires - either as abusers or as addicts - don't have much related to elaborate social plays either, which has me asking the question at hand.<br /> <br /> The average chronicle of Vampire: the Requiem is one that deals with five globe-spanning conspiracies, each with their own beliefs and detailed hierarchies, all intermingling in a Danse Macabre that resembles nothing so much as feudal court. In order for these factions to have any meaning whatsoever (and faces to attach to their varied positions), every domain has to be stuffed with a hilarious number of undead, many of which seem to revolve around their new unholy jobs. <br /> <br /> I have to ask: why? Is this merely inherited from Masquerade, which (as a consequence of its ever-expanding setting via continuous supplements) had to constantly add new details to the point of absurdity? Is there some vital connection to either vampires in general or the themes of the game that this relates to that I'm simply not seeing? For other games, it makes some sense - wolves already run in packs for Werewolf, there are countless stories of wizard schools and secret societies for Mage, and even at the stranger end for Demon you still have spies being tied to wider organizations - yet I see no such connection for vampires. <br /> <br /> I'd love to hear everyone's answers, since this has been stuck in my brain for the last few days.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T20:41:13-07:00","id":859434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me preface this whole topic by saying that this is not meant as a criticism, but rather something of a thought experiment. I adore Requiem and quite like all of the myriad factions and mythology that's been built up for it so far, and have no beef with it continuing.<br /> <br /> What's the &quot;point&quot; of the large focus on elaborate factions, games of reputation and rank, and all the other politics so central to the society of the undead that Requiem revolves around?</div> </div> </div> </div>The focus you're describing is an artifact of <i>Masquerade </i>and the 1st edition. I suggest looking at the 2nd chapter of the <i>Requiem </i>2e core, discarding what you've read either in 1e or <i>Masquerade</i> to come away with a better sense of what Kindred society is actually like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Vampires are, at their core, hungry monsters who were once human, and hold to that former identity with varying degrees of dedication. Most of the vampires we see in folklore, legend, and fiction are largely solitary creatures, with the most famous (Dracula) being sire to four others, none of which he seems to care terribly much for. The immediate and obvious metaphors represented by vampires - either as abusers or as addicts - don't have much related to elaborate social plays either, which has me asking the question at hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, it's worth considering how many elaborate rules are actually in play in a given domain, and whether rules like the Traditions are really that elaborate. Further, I think if you look at the sections describing the All-Night Society in the 2e core, part of the suggestion of the rules is that it helps everyone maintain civility when they're all predators competing for the same resources. On top of that, rules are a big deal for helping you keep up with the Man. The Beast has no rules, no society. Society by Kindred is a constant, difficult test, a little battle of will between Man and Beast.<br /> <br /> Another thing to look at is how the 2e core describes the importance of Vampires keeping company. Without society, without the things it provides for you to do, there is just feeding, and when there's just feeding, there is only the Beast. Engaging in society is a practice for not falling into the inhuman behaviors of low-humanity creatures, which, it just so happens, often resemble many classic vampiric figures. (Banes and the job they do maintaining Humanity is also an important component of how the mechanics of Humanity reflect the narrative of such creatures.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The average chronicle of Vampire: the Requiem is one that deals with five globe-spanning conspiracies, each with their own beliefs and detailed hierarchies, all intermingling in a Danse Macabre that resembles nothing so much as feudal court. In order for these factions to have any meaning whatsoever (and faces to attach to their varied positions), every domain has to be stuffed with a hilarious number of undead, many of which seem to revolve around their new unholy jobs.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is the first misapprehension that fuels this issue. The Covenants are ubiquitous but not globe-spanning conspiracies. They're locally distinguished and localled focused, with few Domains having the luxury or the ability to affect things further than neighboring Domains, and while the 1e Covenant books provide the bevy of positions you're describing, there isn't a great deal of reason to think that these positions are 1. still canon or 2. universally applicable and found.<br /> <br /> As was stated above, part of what the Covenants do is provide things for vampires to do beyond feeding. I don't mean this on an OOC level (Though that is the case as well!) but on an IC one. Vampires who have no code that governs feeding or nothing to do beyond feeding and waiting for the next night are the ones who quickly succumb to the Beast.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have to ask: why? Is this merely inherited from Masquerade, which (as a consequence of its ever-expanding setting via continuous supplements) had to constantly add new details to the point of absurdity?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually the root of many of the ridiculous titles, but rather noticeably, such titles are rarely mentioned in the 2e core, and in the case of the sample settings, is almost always replaced by a local named for the corresponding things.<br /> <br /> Example: Sheriff began as the title of a character in the Chicago book for VtM. It was then retroactively made the title of the chief enforcer of the Camarilla in Vampire domains in <i>Masquerade</i>, and then it made the leap to Requiem's 1e.<br /> <br /> <b>BUT </b>that's why these things aren't really being retained in the second edition so far.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there some vital connection to either vampires in general or the themes of the game that this relates to that I'm simply not seeing?</div> </div> </div> </div>Given that part of your understanding of this is based in Masquerade artifacts from the first edition, you're half right. Cutting away that baggage, the All-Night Society and its rules are an attempt to make vampire society not implode in territorial infighting, and a means of maintaining Humanity with others that understand your condition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For other games, it makes some sense - wolves already run in packs for Werewolf, there are countless stories of wizard schools and secret societies for Mage, and even at the stranger end for Demon you still have spies being tied to wider organizations - yet I see no such connection for vampires.<br /> <br /> I'd love to hear everyone's answers, since this has been stuck in my brain for the last few days.</div> </div> </div> </div>A lone vampire is a crazy lunatic who either drinks people's blood in a cave or acts like a freakish count in the countryside, and both run the risk of attracting hunters, either through the lack of infrastructure to assist with their indiscretions, or through the consequences of dwindling humanity.<br /> <br /> Vampire society is a regulatory body, an exercise in maintaining humanity in the face of isolation and ennui, and an attempt by vampires to find meaning in their existence. All these things are pretty vitally connected to themes of Requiem + Masquerade, Old + New <i>and </i>Piety + Blasphemy. In fact, vampire society is kind of essential for exploring a lot of these ideas, and like the pack of werewolves or the society of wizards, are emergent from the conception of the lone wolfman or the mystical hermit, or in this case, the isolated bloodsucker.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":13}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T20:48:04-07:00","id":859436,"title":"","content":"Well, for one thing, you're under some misconceptions. While the neofeudal court setup is popular and arguably the 'default' setting, it's not nearly the only one. Just a look at Damnation City can show you how other Princes rule and set up their courts, such as the Premier having a senate and limited democracy, the Tyrant having a cult of personality around her random whims, and the Chieftain not really allowing for any organization unless it can challenge him and his childer.<br /> <br /> And of course not everyone does the politicking. The clan books really get this across through many examples. The vampires who get involved in politics usually stick to it strongly, but the average vampire seems to see it as a drag and not something they want to deal with.<br /> <br /> As you pointed out some of the older stories involving vampires don't have them with massive vampiric societies. But the vampires do tend to have a mood of loneliness about them. So if you are not the vengeance-obsessed Dracula, and instead 'just' a normal person, how would you go about alleviating that loneliness? You can try to hang out with humans, but there's always going to be a disconnect. You can reinforce it with magic, make some ghouls, but you'll still have that difference. Just like humans do, vampires seek out other vampires for companionship. Theirs isn't a happy, saccharine or ven ideal social life, but it's better than trying to be friends with the kine.<br /> <br /> And whenever there's a group people, even dead ones, you're going to need some sort of governance. The Carthians might disagree, but still. It starts off small and light, just three traditions, outlining the basic 'rules' of being a vampire that neonates should know just like the living know their pleases and thank yous. But then there's a fight over a ghoul. Someone breaks the Masquerade and the two vampires there are pointing accusingly at each other. Someone has commit diablerie and he's powerful, no one wants to risk their unlife doing anything about it. Or that most egregious of transgressions, you're not like them so they don't like you. So someone decides they'll make that sacrifice. They'll make the decisions, providing others agree to follow them.<br /> <br /> The other titles, they expand from there, sometimes, but the smaller your domain the less you're likely to find. A small town might have a sheriff and no Prince, while New York might have a dozen sheriffs or none, instead having some sort of Lancea abbot-templar. 'Sheriffs' are the cops, the law and order part of the 'standard' court and they come in many different titles and aspects. Most of the other titles are really unofficial. A primogen is often the most powerful member of a clan, they can help you understand what your clan is all about, take responsibility for you if they feel like it, if you've got no sire. A harpy is just someone tuned into the scene, they can know what's up, from new laws and newcomers to the real juicy gossip about who's screwing who. If there's a niche, some people will want to fill it, vampire or not. Most, again, most will likely just stay out of it if they can, the way many real people do, not paying much attention to politics, occasionally breaking the law and trying to hide it, taking advantage of it when they can but otherwise just appreciating the fact that it lets them walk around without worrying about some gang of diablerists kicking in their door.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 04-04-2016, 11:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T20:53:22-07:00","id":859439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859436#post859436\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, for one thing, you're under some misconceptions. While the neofeudal court setup is popular and arguably the 'default' setting, it's not nearly the only one. Just a look at Damnation City can show you how other Princes rule and set up their courts, such as the Premier having a senate and limited democracy, the Tyrant having a cult of personality around her random whims, and the Chieftain not really allowing for any organization unless it can challenge him and his childer.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I think <i>Damnation City </i>is an awesome book in general and the perfect book for pulling off the neo-feudal model, I <i>do </i>think it can be a bit of a bad thing because it's so enslaved by a lexicon that isn't particularly universal or necessary for vampires to have a society.<br /> <br /> Like, use it for inspiration, for sure, hell, use some of the positions, but I think it's really prudent in 2e and onward to recognize that there is not actually a 'default model' of vampire society necessarily. The example Domains do a fantastic job of illustrating that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T20:57:59-07:00","id":859442,"title":"","content":"I was just suggesting it for the variant Princes, who yes, do show off what other domains are like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T00:02:41-07:00","id":859501,"title":"","content":"The way I'm playing Requiem with my players, and I'm nowhere near confident it is as intended to be played, is to experience the personal horror of the &quot;You are the Monster&quot; aspect of the game. There is a lot of overlap with some concepts found also in Masquerade, but all these are examined from the perspectives of the protagonists and the local ecosystem, and how both are scarred by what Vampires can become.<br /> <br /> The political establishment is there as a defense mechanism of the collective undead unconscious, to regulate what sort of trauma surfaces and how it is processed in the preconscious of the All Night Society.<br /> <br /> I am tempted to share my most recent work in a chronicle set in Athens, Greece, to illustrate how I probably get the game wrong. :P The basic premise is that information surfaces on a lost clan or bloodline, established by a nomad from either the Middle East or Africa, came to Greece to lay the groundwork for classical civilization, in an attempt to defend against the Strix (who had supposedly decimated the founder's original society). A Mystery Cult was formed in Athens by some who believed to have found the resting place of a Methuselah from that clan, who claim that line's legacy for Ypochreosi, after they discovered Invictus records of the ancient's rumored resting place. Rituals conducted near the tomb have been stirring the ancient, resulting in an aura which makes politics an explosive matter. Mortal politics are thus intensified inexplicably, drawing crowds to Syntagma square while troika members often leave Greece wondering why the negotiations were so tense and resulted in an uncomfortable deal for all sides with little progress in putting the country's economy back on track.<br /> <br /> This kind of story is, I feel, the major selling point of Requiem over Masquerade. It shows how an entire city has been scarred by events and supernatural energies beyond the control even of the protagonists of those events. It gives you the tools, and allows for new tools to be developed by STs, to create unique monsters of intense drama and horror.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 04-05-2016, 03:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T00:09:03-07:00","id":859505,"title":"","content":"This is all very insightful seeing I have been playing an apolitical character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11528-dangerus-muyoko-project","name":"Dangerus-Muyoko-Project","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T05:09:05-07:00","id":859554,"title":"","content":"There's an old analogy about being Kindred and working in a corporate system.<br /> <br /> You work long hours for little relief, and your coworkers suck. You get promoted by stepping on, betraying, killing and manipulating those around and above you. Your evil, demanding boss is your boss forever, unless something happens to them. And then a new generation of hires come in, and you're in the way of their promotion.<br /> <br /> Play politics or get ashed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2861-nyx","name":"Nyx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2861_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T06:10:43-07:00","id":859576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's the &quot;point&quot; of the large focus on elaborate factions, games of reputation and rank, and all the other politics so central to the society of the undead that Requiem revolves around?</div> </div> </div> </div> To give you something to do. And I'm not just talking about as players. I mean, from an in game perspective, individual vampires have all these factions and games and ranks because, frankly speaking, <i>they're bored</i>. <br /> <br /> Vampire is the closest thing to a game line about immortals we have at the moment. Mummies &quot;live&quot; just as long, but they're asleep for most of it, actually being alive only for a small portion of the time. Prometheans and Unchained demons theoretically can live forever, but their goals (New Dawn, Hell) tend to come with a large turnover / mortality rate. Other splats can extend their lives, but vampire-length lives seems to be the exception rather than rule. That means that, unlike any other game out there for the CofD, Vampire revolves around a very simple question - &quot;What will you do from night to night?&quot; Ennui is a very real threat to Kindred. When you're a neonate, scrambling for blood and survival, the night is pretty darn busy. Once you establish yourself? Nice haven, stable herd and feeding ground, and you catch up on all your reading and Netflix? What's next? Humans, running about their short lives with their families and morgages, simply have different concerns than a vampire. The vampires can't simply take off for a road trip to see Paris or Old Faithful - daylight restrictions. So, they make friends, they try play nice with people that understand them. They find vampire hobbies to fill their time. They take up causes. And those causes clash. The Invictus and Carthians are diametrically opposed for a reason - to create drama. Same with Lance and Crone. The Ordo Dracul is the Wild Card, who side with one or another depending on the current needs of their cult. <br /> <br /> And that's just assuming vampires are acting like humans with just a very long life span. There's also the part where vampires undergo a spiritual transformation. Their base natures have been amplified - vampires are routinely called things like &quot;embodiment of sin&quot; in the core book for a reason. Sexual deviance, the thrill of the hunt, dominance and power, seflishness, an urge to violence, all the ugly parts of humanity become more prominant in vampires. And vampires are always, always hungry for more. Combine that with the daylight traveling restrictions and the need for feeding grounds - no traveling the world for the average vampire. They're effectively trapped in their cities. So, you're someone with the potential to turn into a complete psychopath, bored out of your mind, and the only ones who really -get- you are other territorial and selfish vampires. <br /> <br /> In theory, its possible that vampires could reach an equalibrium, make a society that's both entertaining and balanced. But then, we get the VII and Strix, who very much are designed to upset the status quo and keep pushing vampires to extremes and keep them from settling. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The average chronicle of Vampire: the Requiem is one that deals with five globe-spanning conspiracies, each with their own beliefs and detailed hierarchies, all intermingling in a Danse Macabre that resembles nothing so much as feudal court. </div> </div> </div> Western vampires. After Rome fell, the Invictus and Lance were pretty much the only game in town. They're the ones who made the vampire society like a feudal court. It needs to be noted that in cities where the Invictus does not rule, vampire society does NOT resemble a fuedal court. The Invictus in power tends to be the default, yes, just as vampires in America or Europe tend to be the default setting. But there are variations at play here that differentiate it. Other cities, especially in non-Western cultures, don't follow the same model.<br /> <br /> This is rather important to note, because for those vampires who DON'T want to live in a feudal society? Namely everyone but the Invictus and their oftentimes-allies in the Lance? Are doing their best to bring the system down and replace it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyx</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859554#post859554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's an old analogy about being Kindred and working in a corporate system.<br /> <br /> You work long hours for little relief, and your coworkers suck. You get promoted by stepping on, betraying, killing and manipulating those around and above you. Your evil, demanding boss is your boss forever, unless something happens to them. And then a new generation of hires come in, and you're in the way of their promotion.<br /> <br /> Play politics or get ashed.</div> </div> </div> </div> This is a great description of the Invictus, I'd say. I can't say that I think the same description applies to the other four core book covenants, though. Neither the Carthian Movement nor the Circle have a hierarchy that would work like this, the Ordo system is based on Coils known, oaths sworn, and access to Wyrm Nests and the Lancea et Sanctum, while definitely hierarchal, aren't necessarily power hungry to the point that they'll look to constantly unseat you. The Lance cares about Theban facility, service as a priest or paladin, or some other spiritual function. Sure, there's some friction there, but its generally going to spawn from theological differences rather than coveting power and position for its own sake.<br /> <br /> The Invictus all care about power. Its literally one of the two defining features (the other being the self-entitled keepers of the Masquerade) of the covenant. So, yeah, you're a pool of hungry sharks there. But the other four? Not as much. In fact, its common knowledge that trying to reach positions of power in the Movement or Circle might get you attacked from your own allies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T06:49:59-07:00","id":859598,"title":"","content":"As a point of interest, it'd be interesting to brainstorm some potential replacements for the politics. If we keep the Circle, the Lancea and the Ordo as spiritual/intellectual pursuits, and we get rid of the Carthians and the Invictus, we have room for two Covenants to reflect other things Kindred can do with their unlives that's not politicking. It'd be interesting to have a Covenant of blood warriors dedicated to combating some external threat (and police the Traditions internally), or one dedicated to uplifting mortals, perhaps deriving more Vitae and additional benefits from mortals that they have cared for, or one dedicated to putting vampires' talents for infiltration and stealth to use, perhaps even versus other supernaturals, and so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T07:24:21-07:00","id":859630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowKnight1224</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859598#post859598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a point of interest, it'd be interesting to brainstorm some potential replacements for the politics</div> </div> </div> </div> Not sure why you have to get rid of the Invictus and Carthians to make &quot;room for two more.&quot; Just add or delete covenants - nothing stops you from having more or less than five covenants.<br /> <br /> Also, lots of sample new ones in Danse Macabre. You can also import some of the non-Wester covenants described Ancient Mysteries/Bloodlines books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T07:27:06-07:00","id":859634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859630#post859630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Not sure why you have to get rid of the Invictus and Carthians to make &quot;room for two more.&quot; Just add or delete covenants - nothing stops you from having more or less than five covenants.<br /> <br /> Also, lots of sample new ones in Danse Macabre. You can also import some of the non-Wester covenants described Ancient Mysteries/Bloodlines books.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sure more or less than five Covenants is totally doable, but I did say it'd be interesting to replace the politicking, and that seems to be the main identity of both the Carthians and the Invictus.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T07:31:28-07:00","id":859637,"title":"","content":"I don't think the Invictus is necessarily &quot;the neo-feudal Covenant&quot; either in 2e. Their new writeup emphasizes that they're not stuck in the old ways in this edition. They're still about amassing power, but they've adapted to modern power structures and modern methods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T07:45:52-07:00","id":859646,"title":"","content":"Also, one thing of note - each of the covenants is pretty what we'd imagine clan philosophy to be like. Ventrue Invictus are a natural match for a reason. The Lords are all about winning and dominance, as are the Invictus. The Carthians are all about the power of the masses and beating down their enemies - Daeva specialize in swaying the masses with Majesty, and then fighting back with their Vigor and Celerity. The wild and primal mindset of the Circle of the Crone resonates with the beastial nature of the Gangrel. The Ordo Dracul wanders among its cults and occult signs, and anyone who's read the Mehket clanbook remembers the same obsession with occult symbols and cults that book had. The Lancea and Nosferatu make just as compelling of a match as well, especially with the numberous examples of both having Necropoli, and a focus on scaring people straight.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now, I'm not saying that you should always go for the cliche here. But I am saying that its very likely that each covenant is popular and survives to this day in such numbers is because of the mindset finding such popularity. Each of the five clans represent one aspect of the Curse magnified. Each of the major covenants resonate with a part of every vampire's unlife.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 04-05-2016, 10:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T09:13:19-07:00","id":859685,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859637#post859637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think the Invictus is necessarily &quot;the neo-feudal Covenant&quot; either in 2e. Their new writeup emphasizes that they're not stuck in the old ways in this edition. They're still about amassing power, but they've adapted to modern power structures and modern methods.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Honestly I see them as more &quot;political establishment&quot; which could simply be something that looks like your local crooked city council.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1630-jacob","name":"Jacob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1630_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T09:17:24-07:00","id":859686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859646#post859646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, one thing of note - each of the covenants is pretty what we'd imagine clan philosophy to be like. Ventrue Invictus are a natural match for a reason. The Lords are all about winning and dominance, as are the Invictus. The Carthians are all about the power of the masses and beating down their enemies - Daeva specialize in swaying the masses with Majesty, and then fighting back with their Vigor and Celerity. The wild and primal mindset of the Circle of the Crone resonates with the beastial nature of the Gangrel. The Ordo Dracul wanders among its cults and occult signs, and anyone who's read the Mehket clanbook remembers the same obsession with occult symbols and cults that book had. The Lancea and Nosferatu make just as compelling of a match as well, especially with the numberous examples of both having Necropoli, and a focus on scaring people straight.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty much how I play it using the Bloodlines to spice things up and rupture stereotypes. I do tend to swap the Mekhet and Nosferatu roles from what you have here. I think cults and occult signs works perfectly well for Lancea et Sanctum and necropoli work perfectly well for Ordo Dracul. The 2e rules are plenty flexible enough for all kinds of variations on the themes (which I take to be your point--that I'm riffing on).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1630-jacob","name":"Jacob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1630_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T11:17:59-07:00","id":859769,"title":"","content":"The Invictus will ruthlessly adapt whatever suits them in order to maintain the order and hierarchy of Kindred society. That could be neo-feudal traditions or it might be a corporate style setup, a criminal family's organization or a 'democratic' government whose functions happen in accordance with their desires.<br /> <br /> They're all about maintaining structure, but it doesn't matter what structure that is as long as it reinforces hierarchy. That's the key to understanding the Invictus in 2e.<br /> <br /> It's also important to think about the subtle ways that the Covenants were changed in 2e. 2e reflects a paradigm where not every Covenant in every city is vying to rule said city. The Sanctified as a philosophical position generally prefer someone else to rule while they advise them, for example. It both explains the relationship they have with the Invictus while also giving them a hook for interacting with the other covenants in a way that isn't just competitively trying to assert control.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T15:22:00-07:00","id":859923,"title":"","content":"Interesting. For me, Invictus always screamed &quot;Mafia&quot; first and foremost, and &quot;anything else&quot; second. I didn't read anything for Requiem 1st edition, and never touched any Covenantbook, so my impression is pretty much from detached memories of reading Blood and Smoke when I needed it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T15:47:59-07:00","id":859939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859923#post859923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting. For me, Invictus always screamed &quot;Mafia&quot; first and foremost, and &quot;anything else&quot; second. I didn't read anything for Requiem 1st edition, and never touched any Covenantbook, so my impression is pretty much from detached memories of reading Blood and Smoke when I needed it.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Although, I am familiar with Invictus 1 ed (and the Camarilla from Masquerade), when reading 2ed I defeintly thought crime family and ruthless business executives before the more archaic fuedal stylings. The names are just names, the feel was distinctly modern.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T15:57:49-07:00","id":859943,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859923#post859923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting. For me, Invictus always screamed &quot;Mafia&quot; first and foremost, and &quot;anything else&quot; second. I didn't read anything for Requiem 1st edition, and never touched any Covenantbook, so my impression is pretty much from detached memories of reading Blood and Smoke when I needed it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mafia or Corporation definitely seem like the kind of power structures that the Invictus would seize on as part of the modern zeitgeist. Maybe less so Mafia these days, but they're definitely the two kinds of setups I imagine to be common ways for the Invictus to conceptually hook itself into Kindred society.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T16:02:46-07:00","id":859948,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859943#post859943\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Mafia or Corporation definitely seem like the kind of power structures that the Invictus would seize on as part of the modern zeitgeist. Maybe less so Mafia these days, but they're definitely the two kinds of setups I imagine to be common ways for the Invictus to conceptually hook itself into Kindred society.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Maybe not the Italian Mafia, but the Russian Mob and fairly shady Native American Tribe organizations are pretty prevalent in my neck of the woods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T18:55:10-07:00","id":860036,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=859923#post859923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting. For me, Invictus always screamed &quot;Mafia&quot; first and foremost, and &quot;anything else&quot; second. I didn't read anything for Requiem 1st edition, and never touched any Covenantbook, so my impression is pretty much from detached memories of reading Blood and Smoke when I needed it.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, uh, is it just me or don't those work exactly the same as a feudal system?<br /> <br /> Think about it--the entire point of feudal system is that one ruler subdivides their holdings among others who do the same. An executive director divides the company into divisions with vice presidents, who have managers below them with supervisors and, well, you get it. The mafia is the same with dons, underbosses, territory splits, etc. It's all the same. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T19:25:47-07:00","id":860052,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860036#post860036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, uh, is it just me or don't those work exactly the same as a feudal system?<br /> <br /> Think about it--the entire point of feudal system is that one ruler subdivides their holdings among others who do the same. An executive director divides the company into divisions with vice presidents, who have managers below them with supervisors and, well, you get it. The mafia is the same with dons, underbosses, territory splits, etc. It's all the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>If any hierarchy where delegation of duty and spheres of influence are utilized is defined as neo-feudal, sure.<br /> <br /> I think it's worth considering whether neo-feudal better describes an actual system or if it more appropriately describes the aesthetic of the system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T21:22:59-07:00","id":860102,"title":"","content":"Potential new question: is the average Requiem setting overpopulated?<br /> <br /> There's that old 1:10,000 number I always see tossed about unsourced, meaning that a fairly large city like San Diego has ~140 Kindred in it, while Portland can only have ~61. Sure, London can have nearly a thousand vampires under this metric, but that's at least thousand mortals getting fed upon per week; surely this goes noticed?<br /> <br /> I can't help but wonder if a greater scarcity of Kindred might be more realistic and more entertaining in play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T21:34:22-07:00","id":860108,"title":"","content":"I never bothered with those figures, and I never introduce an NPC that doesn't adds to the story. You're overthinking that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T21:46:40-07:00","id":860119,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860102#post860102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Potential new question: is the average Requiem setting overpopulated?</div> </div> </div> </div>The storyteller populates their settings. So it's up to them to decide if they've overpopulated it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's that old 1:10,000 number I always see tossed about unsourced, meaning that a fairly large city like San Diego has ~140 Kindred in it, while Portland can only have ~61. Sure, London can have nearly a thousand vampires under this metric, but that's at least thousand mortals getting fed upon per week; surely this goes noticed? </div> </div> </div>It's from the first edition core book, and not nearly that simple.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The truth is, Kindred numbers vary from city to city. In most small cities, the proportion of undead to mortals tends to be relatively low: One vampire per 100,000 or more mortals is not uncommon. In large cities, the ratio is usually nearer one vampire per 50,000 mortals. In some cities that seem to draw the Kindred for whatever reason \u2014 cities such as New York, London, New Orleans and Chicago \u2014 the ratio can be substantially higher. Most Princes don\u2019t particularly keep track. As long as the population doesn\u2019t grow so high that people take notice of the predators among them, numbers don\u2019t matter.<br /> <br /> If one were to take a worldwide average, the ratio is probably around the one-to-50,000 mark. It fluctuates so thoroughly from domain to domain that one should never assume that any given population corresponds to that figure. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can't help but wonder if a greater scarcity of Kindred might be more realistic and more entertaining in play. </div> </div> </div>If you feel that way you can have as few vampires within a domain as you want. I don't think I've seen many players think their games were too unrealistic or entertaining due to the number of vampires involved, though your experience may vary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T22:03:22-07:00","id":860124,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860102#post860102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Potential new question: is the average Requiem setting overpopulated?</div> </div> </div> </div>Fiat. We have absolutely no global numbers and absolutely do not need them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860102#post860102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's that old 1:10,000 number I always see tossed about unsourced, meaning that a fairly large city like San Diego has ~140 Kindred in it, while Portland can only have ~61. Sure, London can have nearly a thousand vampires under this metric, but that's at least thousand mortals getting fed upon per week; surely this goes noticed?</div> </div> </div> </div>This is one reason why that metric is a terrible, unusable one and does not make good sense as a rule. Really, it needs to be stressed that it was an average to begin with, and using it as a one size fits all was just not a good idea to begin with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860102#post860102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't help but wonder if a greater scarcity of Kindred might be more realistic and more entertaining in play.</div> </div> </div> </div>The settings never go out of their way to suggest actual numbers, and only really name/engage with certain people in a setting, which I think is the smart way to do it. Vampire skews pretty personal in its stories, and its better for players to have strong feelings about a few key characters than little feelings about a half dozen.<br /> <br /> As an aside, the only valuable discussion of numbers that's ever really occurred is when MachineIV suggested that Tokyo is the most populous vampire domain in the world, so if one has to use metrics, it makes sense to skew smaller in number than the densely packed metropolitan area that supports 34 million people. (I think it was also said that Tokyo particularly lends itself to a large population of vampires because of how the metropolitan area is laid out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T23:19:27-07:00","id":860143,"title":"","content":"So, this is how I think about vampire politics; your mileage will, of course, vary.<br /> <br /> Vampires don't like to talk about it, but they're scared. Like, all the time. Which makes sense, when you think about it. Imagine a world in which, for something like half of every 24 hour period, it rains white hot fire from the sky and the only way to survive is to seal yourself away in a cold dark place and pray that the walls stay standing and nobody opens the front door. On top of that, the entire planet is populated by exactly two types of people: a huge population of fireproof idiots who would have every reason to want you dead if they had any idea you weren't one of them, and a tiny cadre of literally bloodthirsty cannibals who are one bad night away from turning into six-foot tall rabid dogs with super powers.<br /> <br /> The worst part is, you know this is true because you've had nights like that yourself.<br /> <br /> Vampires are like people in all the bad ways. Not <i>only</i> in the bad ways, but in <i>all</i> the bad ways. They can be petty and jealous and conniving and vengeful, and they get into fights for stupid reasons and hold grudges for something resembling eternity.This is only exacerbated by the fact that every vampire domain is basically one of those tiny villages where everybody knows everybody else, and when you do something stupid or embarrassing you can never, ever live it down. And it's not exactly easy to leave because, again, the world is on fire half the time.<br /> <br /> Because of all this interpersonal bullshit, not to mention the risk of frenzying or full-on turning into a soulless monster, vampires can't be sure if they can trust one another not to sell them out to the herd. That joker you humiliated last week at the bar has mind control powers, and he could probably arrange for the vice squad to make a daylight raid on your haven that would put a swift end to your so-called life. He might do it, too; he's looking pretty corpsey these days, and he has a tendency to stare at you the way you've seen hyenas staring down a baby gazelle on nature shows.<br /> <br /> The problem is, if that sort of thing starts happening often enough, pretty soon the mortal population, slow as they collectively are, will start to put the pieces together, and you can bet they're not going to like the picture they see. Somebody has to keep the undead on the straight and narrow, or at least come by every once in a while to smack them upside their collective heads and remind them to keep a low profile. And, of course, there will always be social climbers, people who <i>want</i> to have power and who <i>like</i> to be in charge; in fact, there's at least one whole clan full of them.<br /> <br /> Naturally, this creates a whole lot of new problems, because wherever there's somebody in a position of authority, be it an elementary school teacher or a cop or the President of the United States, some people are going to resent that authority. And if you thought it was bad just hanging around with your fellow vampires, just wait until you're trying to tell them what to do. <br /> <br /> So, what are the leaders of vampire-kind to do to protect them from the people they're trying to lead? Well, one solution is to take a page from monarchs and despots throughout history, and turn your rabble of questionably loyal underlings into a formal court. Make up rituals and rules of etiquette that make it inconvenient for people to plot or strike against you. Reward the biddable with affordable gifts or meaningless honors and titles. Make rules restricting the movement of the powerful in your domain, or which require them to attend certain formal functions that prevent them from spending too much time out of your sight.<br /> <br /> Louis XIV and Tokugawa Ieyasu both used this strategy to great effect, and it may help explain some of the seemingly pointless posturing that goes on in many Kindred courts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":13}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T00:29:08-07:00","id":860160,"title":"","content":"dammit, I lost a whole post talking about this stuff.<br /> <br /> Anyway, look at how the 2e core implies that the Traditions actually represent rules created by vampires that were actually internalized as part of the Kindred Tradition. Look at how the Lost Visage is a component of the Tradition of Masquerade, or how you have an easier time avoiding frenzy while under the Tradition of Elysium.<br /> <br /> In fact, you can actually look at the Ordo Dracul as taking this idea of agency over the curse to an extreme of self modification, and the idea that rules (and hierarchy) can actually be enforced on the condition are factually demonstrable by the Carthians and the Invictus respectively. The rules that the Man sets the Beast are powerful and <i>actually </i>matter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T07:17:54-07:00","id":860287,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860102#post860102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Potential new question: is the average Requiem setting overpopulated?</div> </div> </div> </div>Define &quot;average.&quot; Not only is the average number going to change based on city, but what each individual table will consider overpopulated. The simple truth of the matter is that this is going to depend on what your ST's and group's gaming needs are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's that old 1:10,000 number I always see tossed about unsourced, meaning that a fairly large city like San Diego has ~140 Kindred in it, while Portland can only have ~61. Sure, London can have nearly a thousand vampires under this metric, but that's at least thousand mortals getting fed upon per week; surely this goes noticed? </div> </div> </div> That number came from the 1e Requiem core, but it also came with a caveat - the distribution is not uniform. It was entirely plausable to have 15 Kindred total in the Denver area, but ten times that in New Orleans, and absolutely none in Washington DC. The writers quickly realized that people were using the number as a hard cap on what population &quot;should&quot; be, which was not the intent, and quickly eliminated it from all future games. The number had little meaning in 1e, and absolutely none in 2e.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">I can't help but wonder if a greater scarcity of Kindred might be more realistic and more entertaining in play.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"> *shrugs* I had players that found a &quot;scarcity&quot; of vampires to be less realistic. All depends on the desires of your table and setting in question. What do you want out of the game?</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 04-06-2016, 10:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T07:49:39-07:00","id":860296,"title":"","content":"With regards to the proliferation of positions I think it depends a great deal on how many Covenants are active in a city and how large they are. I would argue that the more Covenants there are in the city the fewer internal positions they're going to have by dint of being a smaller percentage of the overall Kindred population. Not impossible by any means for all five to have entrenched infrastructure and a robust interior hierarchy, but a domain large enough the hold all that would be populous indeed. So say for instance you have Detroit, and Detroit is top to bottom Ordo Dracul. Detroit is going to have a full complement of internal positions within the Ordo in addition to the various city court positions (if they even bother with the latter). Over in DC though where the Ordo is a junior partner in a coalition with the Invictus (hypothetically), they're going to have a smaller share of the population, power, and prestige and will be less likely to have a full suite of titles. Meanwhile in New Orleans where every Covenant is carving off a piece they may barely have anything.<br /> <br /> Alternatively one could also make the argument that the more engaged a Covenant is with the politics of the city the fewer internal positions they will have. It could be a matter of focus and priorities. The difference between being oriented toward the exterior life of the court and the interior life of the Covenant. The Lancea Sanctum may be all but shut out of city affairs and mainly keep to themselves rather than mingle at Elysium, which if they have any numbers also means they have Kindred with nothing better to do than ecumenical politics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T15:39:53-07:00","id":860599,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem#post859421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me preface this whole topic by saying that this is not meant as a criticism, but rather something of a thought experiment. I adore Requiem and quite like all of the myriad factions and mythology that's been built up for it so far, and have no beef with it continuing.<br /> <br /> What's the &quot;point&quot; of the large focus on elaborate factions, games of reputation and rank, and all the other politics so central to the society of the undead that Requiem revolves around?<br /> <br /> Vampires are, at their core, hungry monsters who were once human, and hold to that former identity with varying degrees of dedication. Most of the vampires we see in folklore, legend, and fiction are largely solitary creatures, with the most famous (Dracula) being sire to four others, none of which he seems to care terribly much for. The immediate and obvious metaphors represented by vampires - either as abusers or as addicts - don't have much related to elaborate social plays either, which has me asking the question at hand.<br /> <br /> The average chronicle of Vampire: the Requiem is one that deals with five globe-spanning conspiracies, each with their own beliefs and detailed hierarchies, all intermingling in a Danse Macabre that resembles nothing so much as feudal court. In order for these factions to have any meaning whatsoever (and faces to attach to their varied positions), every domain has to be stuffed with a hilarious number of undead, many of which seem to revolve around their new unholy jobs.<br /> <br /> I have to ask: why? Is this merely inherited from Masquerade, which (as a consequence of its ever-expanding setting via continuous supplements) had to constantly add new details to the point of absurdity? Is there some vital connection to either vampires in general or the themes of the game that this relates to that I'm simply not seeing? For other games, it makes some sense - wolves already run in packs for Werewolf, there are countless stories of wizard schools and secret societies for Mage, and even at the stranger end for Demon you still have spies being tied to wider organizations - yet I see no such connection for vampires.<br /> <br /> I'd love to hear everyone's answers, since this has been stuck in my brain for the last few days.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know very little about Blood&amp;Smoke, but Requiem was pretty vocal about vampires never leaving their hometown. As such, none of the covenants is a &quot;globe-spanning conspiracy&quot;. Being head of Invictus in Vienna means fuck all, because neither you nor your subordinates are ever leaving Vienna.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2347-mahasamatman","name":"Mahasamatman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2347_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T15:42:58-07:00","id":860601,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mahasamatman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860599#post860599\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know very little about Blood&amp;Smoke, but Requiem was pretty vocal about vampires never leaving their hometown. As such, none of the covenants is a &quot;globe-spanning conspiracy&quot;. Being head of Invictus in Vienna means fuck all, because neither you nor your subordinates are ever leaving Vienna.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nomads begs to differ.<br /> <br /> Nevermind how globalization works nowadays.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T15:53:05-07:00","id":860609,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860601#post860601\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nomads begs to differ.<br /> <br /> Nevermind how globalization works nowadays.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nomads are not in the core.<br /> <br /> Has Elder Julius graduated from using the typewriter already?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2347-mahasamatman","name":"Mahasamatman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2347_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T17:23:38-07:00","id":860659,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mahasamatman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860609#post860609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nomads are not in the core.<br /> <br /> Has Elder Julius graduated from using the typewriter already?</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering he just woke out of torpor, he's twittering better than the Council that was set up in the 80's!<br /> <br /> The gothic isolation being described (and the elder technological incompetence) are both pretty soundly phased out in 2e. Refer to the 2nd Chapter of the core for details, but specifically the &quot;Grace of Monsters&quot; artifact and the section on Cloisters for some idea. Neighboring domains <i>definitely </i>talk to one another as is discussed, and the concept of nomads are certainly present. It's not easy for vampires to physically travel, but they have, and evidently have been doing so for millenia, if the existence of the Gallow's Post (discussed in the core and detailed in <i>Requiem for Regina</i>) or the described reason the Post <i>became defunct </i>are anything to go by. This says nothing of the Camarilla, which ruled over the largest swathe of territory ever controlled by one Kindred body, or the Empire described as having existed in Hong Kong, which apparently was the second largest connected network of Kindred domains next to the ancient Roman Camarilla.<br /> <br /> All this comes with the important caveat that no, there is no single governing body of the Lancea et Sanctum or the Ordo Dracul, but there are certainly domains controlled by these covenants that neighbor one another and travel between one another. <i>Secrets of the Covenant </i>will obviously expand on this more, but the playtest merits also refer to travelling and fugitives from different domains and such, so it again is very clearly something that goes on. I wouldn't be surprised by an alliance of say, Invictus domains between a couple of different ones in North America, but they might just share mortal resources between one another, with other vampire domains geographically breaking them up and preventing the formation of a large power bloc that could conceivably threaten others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 04-06-2016, 08:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T07:09:08-07:00","id":861015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=860102#post860102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Potential new question: is the average Requiem setting overpopulated?<br /> <br /> There's that old 1:10,000 number I always see tossed about unsourced, meaning that a fairly large city like San Diego has ~140 Kindred in it, while Portland can only have ~61. Sure, London can have nearly a thousand vampires under this metric, but that's at least thousand mortals getting fed upon per week; surely this goes noticed?<br /> <br /> I can't help but wonder if a greater scarcity of Kindred might be more realistic and more entertaining in play.</div> </div> </div> </div>Having fewer vampires sucks (pun intended), in my opinion, because in low population settnigs it's that much harder to encounter a new vampire the PCs don't already know. In my games, I never, ever, define a specific number of vampires. I don't even estimate it. There is always just the idea that there are more than the PCs know. If there are, say, 40 vampires only, in a city, the PCs might know every one of them. It seriously restricts the setting and it could give more violently inclined vampires the idea that they could theoretically take over the city by force (and hell, with 40 vampires, they probably could). <br /> <br /> In my current game (which has run for roughly 14 or 15 sessions), the PCs know the names of the top ten or so vampires in power, their fuedal lord, another group of five vampires in an adjoining territory, and three scattered other vampires. That's it. Now, they've been in the presence of at least three dozen other vampires, but they don't know their names, nor have they had meaningful interaction with them. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T07:38:19-07:00","id":861030,"title":"","content":"In my games, there's always enough vampires for the PCs not know everyone by name, but a more social character might recognize anyone by association (&quot;Don't know him, but he hangs out with such-and-such&quot;).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4854-justin-sane","name":"Justin Sane","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T09:45:13-07:00","id":861106,"title":"","content":"My take on things:<br /> <br /> First of all, vampires are creatures of appetite. For blood, first and foremost -- but also for power, respect, revenge, occult knowledge... The elaborate social games and hierarchies of the Covenants are a way to feed those more abstract hungers in a relatively controlled manner. All the elaborate protocol and ritual means that vampires have to act with subtlety when pursuing their ambitions. Vampiric political life acts as a filter to weed out the most reckless before they have a chance to seriously endanger the status quo.<br /> <br /> Secondly, the All-Night Society in general and the Covenants in particular help vampires pretend they're still people, as opposed to being soulless parasites. Socializing with other vampires can be risky, certainly, but it offers different risks than opening up to a human. Other Kindred can understand what you're going through in a way no-one else can. As for the Covenants, they offer comforting ideological justifications for your otherwise horrifying existence. It's so much easier to stay sane when you can convince yourself that you're a part of the natural order, or God's chosen avenger, or that you might one day transcend your damnation and become truly superhuman.<br /> <br /> Finally, there's safety in numbers. Mortal hunters are a real threat to a lone vampire, and the Strix -- and stranger things -- are always out there. Additionally, Covenant status or the favor of powerful Kindred are powerful defenses if another vampire goes after you or your herd. The more contacts you have, the more likely one of them will give a shit if you're in it deep. They'll probably expect something in return, of course, but every vampire knows there's no such thing as a free lunch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/40-dspaul","name":"DSPaul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T11:15:39-07:00","id":861166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justin Sane</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861030#post861030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my games, there's always enough vampires for the PCs not know everyone by name, but a more social character might recognize anyone by association (&quot;Don't know him, but he hangs out with such-and-such&quot;).</div> </div> </div> </div>How's that working out with the Predator's Taint rules?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2347-mahasamatman","name":"Mahasamatman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2347_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T11:23:05-07:00","id":861171,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mahasamatman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861166#post861166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How's that working out with the Predator's Taint rules?</div> </div> </div> </div>Predators aura changes the taint. It very nice. One of the better revisements.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T11:53:27-07:00","id":861184,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861015#post861015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having fewer vampires sucks (pun intended), in my opinion, because in low population settnigs it's that much harder to encounter a new vampire the PCs don't already know. In my games, I never, ever, define a specific number of vampires. I don't even estimate it. There is always just the idea that there are more than the PCs know. If there are, say, 40 vampires only, in a city, the PCs might know every one of them. It seriously restricts the setting and it could give more violently inclined vampires the idea that they could theoretically take over the city by force (and hell, with 40 vampires, they probably could).<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I can see what you mean. My game is structured differently. My principle player in the setting is the Sheriff, childe of the Prince and very curious Mekhet. I don't feel good about retconning new characters all the time (&quot;The guy facing you -- ah, yeah, you've seen him at Elysium for the last decade, but never interacted much with him&quot;). So, she has the names of all the acknowledged Kindred in the city. I still have room to develop most of them -- many are not fleshed out beyond names, pictures and some very general notes. Also, that there are a number of vampires in the city that fly underneath the radar -- those who live street-level until the Sheriff or the Scourge notices them. Plus, there are a few staked vamps from the previous regime that can be pulled out of storage when there is a need. Finally, someone can come in from outside the city, visiting, bearing messages, or attempting to immigrate. <br /> <br /> So, I can't do anything resembling &quot;murder-of-the-week&quot; procedurals because there aren't any faceless mooks among the Kindred. It does raise the the tension when something untoward does happen, however. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T13:26:02-07:00","id":861252,"title":"","content":"My key view on politics in Vampire is that Kindred are nasty, territorial predators, and without a political framework to hold them into check, they'd never interact except to fight. I remember way back when I first heard there was a game about vampires in the works, and my first thought was &quot;how is that going to be interesting?&quot; Here we are 26 years later...<br /> <br /> Despite being a legacy of Masquerade, the politics are a lot more personal, or should be IMHO, than the previous conceit. VtM (and really, most of the oWoD's societies) was monolithic and rigid and oppressive. It was rife with eldritch conspiracies, ancient masters who could devour cities, and parallel caste systems. Requiem is much more personal and intersectional, and it has more to do with how you live as a vampire, rather than trying to survive the Jyhad. Conspiracies here are hatched in cloakrooms and around corners, rather than in antediluvian tombs or soaring skyscrapers, and it's too easy to catch up mortals in the schemes, who aren't the faceless Kine of old. Everyone matters in Requiem, as people, and even more so in 2E, and it's politics that will save your Touchstone from your rivals, not bloodyminded violence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T13:51:44-07:00","id":861267,"title":"","content":"On the subject of population, I've had the experience of being in a game where there were too few vampires and it made things feel silly. I was playing a game of VtM in Toronto, roughly 1800's. There were fourteen vampires in the whole city. At the same time, we had a Prince, a Senshal, a Sheriff, a Scourge, and primogen seats for all 7 clans. There were more titles than untitled vampires. It felt silly when I claimed to be the primogen of the Tremere, I was the only one. For me, I don't even feel like there is a 'clan presence' or a 'covenent presence' until there are at least three members, preferably more. Until then, it's just an individual or two.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1744-thantrax","name":"Thantrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1744_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T16:52:45-07:00","id":861429,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mahasamatman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861166#post861166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How's that working out with the Predator's Taint rules?</div> </div> </div> </div>As expected. The social butterfly doesn't have to worry that much about it (which makes him the perfect person to spot outsiders), the shut-in gets really nervous every now and then.<br /> <br /> Although there was that one time that a NPC used Obfuscate to masquerade as a ghoul. Fun times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4854-justin-sane","name":"Justin Sane","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T18:18:47-07:00","id":861482,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thantrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861267#post861267\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the subject of population, I've had the experience of being in a game where there were too few vampires and it made things feel silly. I was playing a game of VtM in Toronto, roughly 1800's. There were fourteen vampires in the whole city. At the same time, we had a Prince, a Senshal, a Sheriff, a Scourge, and primogen seats for all 7 clans. There were more titles than untitled vampires. It felt silly when I claimed to be the primogen of the Tremere, I was the only one. For me, I don't even feel like there is a 'clan presence' or a 'covenent presence' until there are at least three members, preferably more. Until then, it's just an individual or two.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that was mostly a consequence of how Masquerade lowballed the Camarilla numbers for most of their established settings, though I can see how the issue could crop up when people think VtR should function the same way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T22:53:38-07:00","id":861612,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861184#post861184\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My principle player in the setting is the Sheriff, childe of the Prince and very curious Mekhet.</div> </div> </div> </div>How did your PCs rise to such positions of power? Did you just start them there? If one is the Sheriff, what are the other PCs? That sounds like a very different kind of game...when my PCs get positions like Sheriff, it's usually because another one of them is Prince, because they've torn the whole damn system down and started fresh with the lot of them at the top.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T07:12:47-07:00","id":861806,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861612#post861612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How did your PCs rise to such positions of power? Did you just start them there? If one is the Sheriff, what are the other PCs? That sounds like a very different kind of game...when my PCs get positions like Sheriff, it's usually because another one of them is Prince, because they've torn the whole damn system down and started fresh with the lot of them at the top.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds like most of your games play more or less the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T08:33:55-07:00","id":861853,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861612#post861612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How did your PCs rise to such positions of power? Did you just start them there? If one is the Sheriff, what are the other PCs? That sounds like a very different kind of game...when my PCs get positions like Sheriff, it's usually because another one of them is Prince, because they've torn the whole damn system down and started fresh with the lot of them at the top.</div> </div> </div> </div>PCs in positions of power is extremely common in my experience. Then again, my experience is primarily LARP.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T08:54:44-07:00","id":861869,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861853#post861853\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> PCs in positions of power is extremely common in my experience. Then again, my experience is primarily LARP.</div> </div> </div> </div>It does tend to be a bit more common in LARP than in tabletop, though it's definitely not unknown in the latter. At least in my experience. When I'm running a game my preference tends to be to place the PCs in low rung and informal court positions. Just enough authority to give them some push with NPCs, a reason to be involved in the goings on, and an access point into city politics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:22:06-07:00","id":861944,"title":"","content":"Yes, some of my players in the past have LARPed, and yeah, all of the positions are held by PCs. But in a normal table top game? Players don't get to just start at 100 XP or whatever it takes to be a major political player. I mean, it's an option, but it didn't seem common. The default, 0 XP vampire with 7 humanity that the game assumes...there's no way that guy is anyone of note in the city. Even if she's the Prince's childe, there's no way she takes a major position right away. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861806#post861806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sounds like most of your games play more or less the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have run two games, out of dozens, where the PCs ripped the government down entirely and rebuilt it in their image. After more than a year of play each time. Now, yes, in the game I'm currently running, ripping down the government is kind of the point, so, that will likely be the end here as well, but In most others? They accomplish their goals, whatever those may be, usually involving stabilizing the city in some fashion, and then, yeah, the Prince stays in power, or a new one takes over, but it's still not a PC. Having run it a couple of times, it's really not super fun to run or play game where the PCs are in charge. There's just not a lot for them to do, day to day. There's just so much time dilation and almost nothing is a threat...again, though, this goes for table top games. Its my impression of LARP from friends who are totally absorbed in it that you basically hang out and talk to people in character, as if it were a regular party but you're dressed funny, then all of the actual vampire action that is what would constitute a normal table top game happens through complex e-mail chains and forum posts. That's just such a totally different experience, and I can imagine having fun running city via e-mail, but trust me, it's not super fun at the table. At least, not for people looking for immersion in their characters (rather than the setting).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:44:19-07:00","id":861959,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861944#post861944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, some of my players in the past have LARPed, and yeah, all of the positions are held by PCs. But in a normal table top game? Players don't get to just start at 100 XP or whatever it takes to be a major political player. I mean, it's an option, but it didn't seem common. The default, 0 XP vampire with 7 humanity that the game assumes...there's no way that guy is anyone of note in the city. Even if she's the Prince's childe, there's no way she takes a major position right away.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you can easily start with City Status 5 and Covenant Status 5. And &quot;The Prince's Childe&quot; is a legitimate character concept in my opinion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861944#post861944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have run two games, out of dozens, where the PCs ripped the government down entirely and rebuilt it in their image. After more than a year of play each time. Now, yes, in the game I'm currently running, ripping down the government is kind of the point, so, that will likely be the end here as well, but In most others? They accomplish their goals, whatever those may be, usually involving stabilizing the city in some fashion, and then, yeah, the Prince stays in power, or a new one takes over, but it's still not a PC. Having run it a couple of times, it's really not super fun to run or play game where the PCs are in charge. There's just not a lot for them to do, day to day. There's just so much time dilation and almost nothing is a threat...again, though, this goes for table top games. Its my impression of LARP from friends who are totally absorbed in it that you basically hang out and talk to people in character, as if it were a regular party but you're dressed funny, then all of the actual vampire action that is what would constitute a normal table top game happens through complex e-mail chains and forum posts. That's just such a totally different experience, and I can imagine having fun running city via e-mail, but trust me, it's not super fun at the table. At least, not for people looking for immersion in their characters (rather than the setting).</div> </div> </div> </div>Man, your LARP friends definitely didn't play in Olympia, WA. We did have that &quot;hanging out, talking in character like it's a normal party&quot; experience from night to night, but the action was not all in email chains and forum posts. ...Well, the one side quest where the Prince, the Sheriff, the Daeva Priscus, and the Invictus Primogen all went to Mexico to track down an elder who might have been connected with VII did, but that was an exception. The journey into the Underworld led by a Moros to find the Norns and entreat them for their eye happened at location, as did the initial VII attack that set the whole thing off.<br /> <br /> ...Aaaaanywhoo, I certainly have fun in games with influential NPCs in table top. It seems like you and I have very different ideas about what constitutes immersion","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:53:27-07:00","id":861966,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861959#post861959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;The Prince's Childe&quot; is a legitimate character concept in my opinion.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is the concept behind signature characters Persephone Moore and the Mogul.<br /> <br /> There's nothing wrong with starting a character with status and titles. Straight out of character generation you may not have abilities to back it up very well but not everyone starts with those same rules, and it's still doable considering how the political system works, since titles don't go to the people with the highest numbers on their character sheet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T11:45:32-07:00","id":862020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861959#post861959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, you can easily start with City Status 5 and Covenant Status 5. And &quot;The Prince's Childe&quot; is a legitimate character concept in my opinion.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but that character can't realistically be the Sheriff or whatever. They'd be hilariously inept. They would get wrecked by the first real threat, or the Prince would have to show how stupid and weak he is by sending goons (who should be the Sheriff instead) to help the Sheriff he propped up through nepotism. Being the Prince's Childe is a totally fine concept, but that doesn't automatically imply real titles or power.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861959#post861959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Man, your LARP friends definitely didn't play in Olympia, WA.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope, the one guy is a major player in the national scene, but specifically plays in Philly/South Jersey, and travels a shit ton for it to conventions and other realms. But he spends more time posting on LARP forums and sending LARP e-mails than he does actually working his real job. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861959#post861959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...Aaaaanywhoo, I certainly have fun in games with influential NPCs in table top. It seems like you and I have very different ideas about what constitutes immersion</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we do, yes. But besides that, I'm curious...what kind of stuff do your influential PCs (I assume that was a typo) do at the table top? They are established...they don't have to hunt, really, by that point. They control the human city they live in, so, they can solve most problems with a phone call. They have insane terrifying discipline spreads or friends who have those and for whatever reason don't want to be the boss (because otherwise, the guys with those spreads would have ousted them already).<br /> <br /> Confucius described the ideal empire as revolving around the emperor. They sit in a seat of luxury and everything around them just works. The whole point of becoming the boss is so you don't have to work anymore--you delegate everything. Or you roflstomp your problems because you wouldn't be the boss if you couldn't. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861966#post861966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">titles don't go to the people with the highest numbers on their character sheet.</div> </div> </div> </div>Realistically, how could they not? I would consider it a plot point whenever that <i>didn't</i> happen, because said weak title holder would be a major vulnerability. If the Sheriff can't beat up the criminals, how can they do their job? If they need help to do their job, how badly does that reflect on the Prince who appointed them? Why wouldn't the ones helping just take over the Sheriff position anyway? A decision like that would rock the political landscape. When weak people are on top, the system starts to fall apart. <br /> <br /> Now, you can run a game about that--I have run several. But if a PC is the weak link in everything? I don't know, I feel like being &quot;the Meg&quot; that ruins everything would feel pretty shitty and lead to a rather unfun game for that player.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T13:26:51-07:00","id":862147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=862020#post862020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Realistically, how could they not? I would consider it a plot point whenever that <i>didn't</i> happen, because said weak title holder would be a major vulnerability. If the Sheriff can't beat up the criminals, how can they do their job? If they need help to do their job, how badly does that reflect on the Prince who appointed them? Why wouldn't the ones helping just take over the Sheriff position anyway? A decision like that would rock the political landscape. When weak people are on top, the system starts to fall apart.</div> </div> </div> </div>You misread or misunderstand. I said titles don't go to the people with the highest numbers on their character sheet. I did not say they only go to people with the lowest numbers on their character sheet. Providing there are more than two people in your game, there's going to be people between them.<br /> <br /> Realistically, how does it happen? The same way they happen in real life. Do you think every ruler or boss in the history of mankind was the person most capable of doing the job? Or are you willing to accept that in many cases, compromises are made? That cronyism and nepotism exist? That the strongest people in the world aren't itching to become bodyguards for people they don't know or like. Or that the smartest people in the world might be more interested in their own thing than serving someone else? The idea of a Prince being put in place because a more favorable option would lead to war is common in Vampire. A Sheriff who thinks they can beat up everyone else, and suddenly has the right to, is not necessarily the best person for the job. Especially in the political world, where someone may be diametrically opposed to you politically and giving them a title where their job is to be as social as possible is asking for failure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T13:48:36-07:00","id":862168,"title":"","content":"Puppet governments are a thing. Just because you're <i>in power</i> doesn't mean you <i>have power</i>.<br /> <br /> Edit: Come to think about it, this is pretty much how every regime ever has ended. Although there are exceptions if one bothers to examine the historical record of our Earth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1630-jacob","name":"Jacob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1630_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T14:45:20-07:00","id":862198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=862147#post862147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Realistically, how does it happen? The same way they happen in real life.</div> </div> </div> </div>In real life, the disgruntled underling who was looked over for promotion can't mind control the incompetent guy above him, or rip his face off, or invisibly ruin his life, or tons of other things supernatural powers can do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=862147#post862147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That cronyism and nepotism exist?</div> </div> </div> </div>They totally exist. But in the real world, the lower orders of talented people have no outlet. Being really smart in the real world doesn't give you mind control or invisibility. Being a great salesman doesn't let you read minds or create illusions. Super powers change everything.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T14:59:35-07:00","id":862204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=862198#post862198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In real life, the disgruntled underling who was looked over for promotion can't mind control the incompetent guy above him, or rip his face off, or invisibly ruin his life, or tons of other things supernatural powers can do.</div> </div> </div> </div>They can't even do it in Vampire. Not every vampire is a cunning mastermind. Mind control isn't always guaranteed, and ripping faces off certainly isn't. And, more importantly, not everyone wants a title.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They totally exist. But in the real world, the lower orders of talented people have no outlet. Being really smart in the real world doesn't give you mind control or invisibility. Being a great salesman doesn't let you read minds or create illusions. </div> </div> </div>And being a master mind controller doesn't mean you would be a good Prince, or even want to be one. A great salesman in life might want to continue being a great salesman in undeath, and not use their abilities to promote the Prince as a harpy. Because, again, they might be diametrically opposed to one another.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Super powers change everything. </div> </div> </div>Some of the more in-depth posts responding to atamajakki's question have pointed out that super powers <i>don't</i> change everything.<br /> <br /> You have more tools at your disposal, but that doesn't mean you'll be a great ruler. One vampire might be able to kill a dozen others without taking a shot, but he might be a horrible investigator, or simply not want the job. Then there's the subfactions, who might have to take what they can get. And blood bonding, which a superior can use to keep people beneath them in line.<br /> <br /> One of the best examples of this in Requiem's vast setting, to me, is Billy Vines. He made a deal with a demon and can, without his vampiric might dimmed at all, walk freely in the sun. He has ~12 hours more to work with than every other vampire around, can use his powers to walk into the home of an enemy, or someone who outranks him, and kill them. But he doesn't have the ability to leverage that into power. If he acts too much during the day, other Kindred will catch on, and he'll find himself destroyed for the danger he risks. And where would he start? There's no guarantee killing anyone would get him anything. He's still no more charismatic than he was, no more intelligent or witty. He has another tool at his disposal and can see no way to use it.<br /> <br /> Yes, we as people who have the benefit of knowing the rulebooks and how things work can. But Billy is not an out-of-world character who has the benefit of being able to look at it from our angle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T17:53:07-07:00","id":862309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=861612#post861612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How did your PCs rise to such positions of power? Did you just start them there? If one is the Sheriff, what are the other PCs? That sounds like a very different kind of game...when my PCs get positions like Sheriff, it's usually because another one of them is Prince, because they've torn the whole damn system down and started fresh with the lot of them at the top.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an odd turn of the conversation. It's a long story, both IC and OOC. I'm just not sure why it seems an offensive idea to you. My first ST chief delight was in keeping the PCs small and shat upon, and I try not to emulate him in the games I run. So, yes, I have had run for PCs of both low and high station, as interests and circumstances dictate. It is entirely possible that a game spanning 30 years in-game and 7 IRL could see someone earn a position of power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-09T08:59:22-07:00","id":862598,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=862309#post862309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's an odd turn of the conversation. It's a long story, both IC and OOC. I'm just not sure why it seems an offensive idea to you. My first ST chief delight was in keeping the PCs small and shat upon, and I try not to emulate him in the games I run. So, yes, I have had run for PCs of both low and high station, as interests and circumstances dictate. It is entirely possible that a game spanning 30 years in-game and 7 IRL could see someone earn a position of power.</div> </div> </div> </div>The idea is not offensive, just unusual and interesting. But, yeah, wow, revised question, what is there left to do in a game that's run 7 years?! How have you kept that going? That's amazing.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-09T12:15:54-07:00","id":862687,"title":"","content":"I feel that some of the &quot;players in position of power, how?&quot; thing stems from tendency to have XP loaded npcs running around. One of things that bought me in Requiem was the fact that I could totally run a city where there is no vampire at BP more than, like, 5, and probably almost noone whose unlife is actually longer than their life. <br /> I know that there are ways and tricks for Really Elder Elders to sustain their clutch on the setting, but I really liked the fact that I could just say &quot;nope, they go to sleep and wake up some time later with reduced BP&quot;. Not a fan of deeply entrenched super powerful characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-09T12:28:59-07:00","id":862696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/859421-what-s-the-point-of-politics-in-requiem?p=862598#post862598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea is not offensive, just unusual and interesting. But, yeah, wow, revised question, what is there left to do in a game that's run 7 years?! How have you kept that going? That's amazing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, first off, it's not a weekly game. Probably averages to a monthly, with a fair bit of bluebooking in between. Second, because we live in the middle of nowhere, it's hard to get players. Therefore, the game has generally centered around one player (the sheriff), and most of the games are solos. <br /> <br /> What's left to do? There is a great deal to cover in a city over the years, and many problems are ongoing or recurring. <br /> <br /> There are her duties to the city -- unacknowledged kindred are not tolerated, but any city can have quite a few scattered around. She is tasked with keeping the peace with the local werewolves, simply because she's dealt with them and survived in the past. She is obsessed with security -- from the hostile Kindred across the river, from Hunters, and from VII who cleaned out the city just before she was Embraced some decades ago. She gathers intel on everyone in the city -- including her sire, the Prince. She cultivates a network of informants both Kindred and mortal. <br /> <br /> There are her duties to the Covenant -- she has made quite a name for herself in the Ordo with her study of VII, plus she is a mentor to one or two younger members. She is eager to learn more about the spirit world (sometimes drawing on the spirituality of her own First Nations ancestry) and her own bloodline. She also must balance strengthening the Dragons against the other covenants with keeping the order and security of the city.<br /> <br /> She has to make money, and here that isn't run simply through dots on a page. She keeps up a number of criminal contacts in order to raise capital, and has to deal with law enforcement to keep those lines of supply open. <br /> <br /> She has a number of ghouls and dependents of ghouls to keep an eye on. Though she may see them as friends or employees, they are also tools, and tools must be maintained and upgraded.<br /> <br /> In a given session, there may be a couple of things going on-- looking for a diablerist, and convincing a gang leader to supply his weed to her network. Or overseeing security for a major Ellysium while riding herd on a student at the Ordo Academy. <br /> <br /> This is part of why it is imperative for me to have all the known vampires named: the PC has history and relationships with a good many of them. When Myron the Daeva Invictus betrays the city by letting in an outside faction of Lancea, he's not just a threat of the week for the sheriff to deal with. He's someone Sheriff Victoria traded barbs with for 20 years; she manipulated Myron into embracing without permission, surveiled his ghouls, and orchestrated his fall from the top spot in the local Invictus. She saw the evolution of his hate-on with the Circle of the Crone (and helped it along, after a fashion) which culminated in his calling for forces outside the covenant and city to deal with the problem. The consequences to the city and to the traitor had a lot more resonance for the player than it otherwise would have. <br /> <br /> So, yes, lots to do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}